  "content": {
    "id": "p105-tf-question",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "True or False?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p105-tf-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "If $a\\geq 0$, then $x^2+y^2=a$ is a circle.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False for a non-degenerate circle: $a>0$ is required. When $a=0$, the graph is the single point $(0,0)$, a degenerate circle.",
          "worked": "For $a>0$, the equation represents a circle centred at the origin with radius $\\sqrt{a}$. When $a=0$, $x^2+y^2=0$ has only the solution $(0,0)$, so its radius is zero. The statement is false when “circle” means a non-degenerate circle.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p105-tf-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "The graph of $x^2+y^2=36$ represents a function.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "At $x=0$, the equation gives $y^2=36$, so $y=6$ or $y=-6$. One $x$-value gives two $y$-values, so the graph fails the vertical line test and does not represent a function.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p105-tf-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p105-tf-c-opening",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "For the circle with equation",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p105-tf-c-equation",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "center",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "x^2+y^2+ax+by+c=0,",
                  "display": true
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p105-tf-c-conclusion",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "the value of ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "c",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " does not affect the centre.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True. The centre is $\\left(-\\frac{a}{2},-\\frac{b}{2}\\right)$.",
          "worked": "Complete the square in both variables.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+ax+y^2+by+c&=0\\\\\\left(x+\\frac{a}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{a^2}{4}+\\left(y+\\frac{b}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{b^2}{4}+c&=0\\\\\\left(x+\\frac{a}{2}\\right)^2+\\left(y+\\frac{b}{2}\\right)^2&=\\frac{a^2+b^2}{4}-c\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe centre is $\\left(-\\frac{a}{2},-\\frac{b}{2}\\right)$, which is independent of $c$. Thus the statement is true. For values that give a circle, changing $c$ changes the radius.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      }
    ]
  },
